WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.697
Associative containers can make lookup logic safer and clearer.

2
00:00:03.697 --> 00:00:12.734
A map, set, unordered_map, or unordered_set can express that the program is organizing data by key instead of scanning ad hoc structures.

3
00:00:12.734 --> 00:00:15.610
That clarity depends on correct key semantics.

4
00:00:15.610 --> 00:00:18.074
Ordered containers rely on comparator behavior.

5
00:00:18.074 --> 00:00:25.879
At a practical level, the comparator must provide a consistent ordering so the container can maintain its internal structure.

6
00:00:25.879 --> 00:00:32.452
If ordering depends on changing external state or inconsistent rules, lookup and insertion behavior becomes unreliable.

7
00:00:32.452 --> 00:00:35.738
Unordered containers rely on hash and equality consistency.

8
00:00:35.738 --> 00:00:39.846
Values considered equal should behave consistently with the hash policy.

9
00:00:39.846 --> 00:00:46.007
If equality and hashing disagree, lookups may fail in surprising ways and review becomes difficult.

10
00:00:46.007 --> 00:00:49.704
Externally influenced keys and item counts need resource limits.

11
00:00:49.704 --> 00:00:55.045
A service that stores every distinct key it receives can grow without bound.

12
00:00:55.045 --> 00:01:00.795
Limits, quotas, expiration, aggregation, or rejection policies may be needed before data is inserted.

13
00:01:00.795 --> 00:01:04.903
Reserve behavior, load factors, and rehashing affect resources and invalidation.

14
00:01:04.903 --> 00:01:10.243
Reserving space can improve predictability, but it should be based on validated sizes.

15
00:01:10.243 --> 00:01:13.530
Rehashing can invalidate iterators and change performance characteristics.

16
00:01:13.530 --> 00:01:16.405
Key design should also minimize sensitive data.

17
00:01:16.405 --> 00:01:21.745
Storing raw external identifiers as keys may create privacy, retention, or logging concerns.

18
00:01:21.745 --> 00:01:27.496
A safer internal key representation can make lookup behavior clearer while reducing unnecessary exposure.

19
00:01:27.496 --> 00:01:30.372
Review lookup structures for correctness and pressure.

20
00:01:30.372 --> 00:01:40.230
Ask what can create entries, how many entries are allowed, when entries are removed, whether key comparison is stable, and how the container behaves

21
00:01:40.230 --> 00:01:41.873
under expected production load.

22
00:01:41.873 --> 00:01:45.981
Do not let convenience turn associative containers into unbounded registries.

23
00:01:45.981 --> 00:01:53.786
If entries represent sessions, requests, files, users, or external records, define expiration, eviction, ownership, and monitoring rules that match

24
00:01:53.786 --> 00:01:55.840
the system's reliability goals.
